      content:
          'A 48-year-old male is admitted to the inpatient rehabilitation unit 6 weeks after a basilar artery occlusion resulting in a ventral pontine infarction. He is fully conscious and cognitively intact but has complete quadriplegia and anarthria. His only voluntary movements are vertical eye movements and blinking. He was initially misdiagnosed as being in a vegetative state in the ICU for 9 days before a neurologist identified preserved consciousness through eye blink responses. He communicates through a partner-assisted eye-blink code (one blink = yes, two blinks = no). His wife and 14-year-old daughter are at bedside. He has a tracheostomy and is ventilator-dependent. He has a PEG tube for nutrition.',
