    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **Event**: Sudden onset vertigo, dysarthria, and progressive quadriparesis over 6 hours. Presented to ED where CT angiography revealed basilar artery occlusion. Attempted endovascular thrombectomy was unsuccessful. MRI confirmed extensive ventral pontine infarction bilaterally with sparing of the tegmentum (preserving consciousness and vertical eye movements).\n- **ICU Course**: Intubated for airway protection. Initially assessed as \'unresponsive\' — documented as \'comatose\' for 9 days. Wife repeatedly told staff he was \'in there\' because she noticed he would blink when she spoke. Neurology consultation on day 9 confirmed locked-in syndrome via structured eye-blink assessment.\n- **Tracheostomy**: Performed day 12. Currently ventilator-dependent (AC mode, FiO2 30%, PEEP 5, RR 14). Tolerates tracheostomy collar trials for 30-60 minutes.\n- **Nutrition**: PEG tube placed day 14. Tube feeds at goal rate. NPO — video fluoroscopic swallow study showed absent pharyngeal swallow initiation.\n- **Neurological Status**: Cognition fully intact — scored 28/30 on a modified MMSE administered via eye-blink responses (errors were due to communication lag, not cognitive deficit). Vertical eye movements and eyelid opening/closing are the only voluntary motor functions. No horizontal eye movements. No facial movement. No limb movement. No head or trunk control.\n- **Medical Complications**: Two UTIs (indwelling catheter). One episode of atelectasis requiring bronchoscopy. DVT prophylaxis with enoxaparin.\n- **Pre-morbid**: Software engineer. Married 18 years. Daughter is an honor student. Avid cyclist. No prior medical history except hypertension diagnosed 2 years ago — had stopped taking lisinopril 6 months prior to stroke.\n- **Medications**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily, pantoprazole 40 mg via PEG, acetaminophen 1000 mg via PEG Q8H, docusate 100 mg via PEG BID, tube feeds (Osmolite 1.2 at 75 mL/hr).',
