      content:
          '**Vitals**: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14 (ventilator), SpO2 98% on 30% FiO2, Temp 37.1C.\n\n**General**: Alert male in hospital bed with tracheostomy connected to ventilator. Eyes open and tracking the examiner. Blinks purposefully in response to questions.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Consciousness**: Fully alert. Follows complex commands via eye blinks. Demonstrates humor — blinks \'no\' emphatically when asked if the hospital food is good (he cannot eat).\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Vertical eye movements intact — full upgaze and downgaze. Horizontal eye movements absent bilaterally. Pupils equal, reactive. No facial movement (CN VII bilateral paralysis). No jaw movement. No tongue movement. Corneal reflexes absent bilaterally.\n- **Motor**: 0/5 in all four extremities. No voluntary movement in any muscle group below the eyes. Diffuse flaccidity. No head control. No trunk control.\n- **Sensory**: Intact — blinks to indicate he feels light touch and pinprick in all extremities (confirmed by systematic testing with yes/no blink responses). Reports pain in bilateral shoulders via eye-blink pain assessment.\n- **Reflexes**: Bilateral Babinski signs. Hyperreflexia in all extremities (3+). No clonus.\n\n**Respiratory**: Ventilator-dependent. Breath sounds clear bilaterally. Tracheostomy site clean. Tolerates 30-60 minute collar trials.\n\n**Skin**: Stage 1 pressure injury over sacrum (non-blanchable erythema). Heels intact with offloading boots in place.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Bilateral shoulder pain with passive ROM (blinks rapidly indicating pain at 80 degrees flexion bilaterally). Early contracture formation — bilateral ankle plantar flexion contractures (dorsiflexion limited to -10 degrees). Bilateral elbow flexion contractures (extension limited to -15 degrees).',
