    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the AAC assessment and technology options for a patient with locked-in syndrome whose only voluntary movements are vertical eye movements and blinking.',
      content:
          '**AAC Assessment for Locked-In Syndrome**:\n\n**Step 1: Characterize Residual Motor Function**\n- This patient has: vertical eye movements (upgaze, downgaze), eyelid opening/closing (blink)\n- He does NOT have: horizontal eye movements, facial movement, limb movement, phonation, head movement\n- Sensation is intact — proprioceptive and tactile feedback channels are available\n- Cognition is intact — he can learn complex systems\n\n**Step 2: Low-Technology AAC (Immediate)**:\n- **Partner-assisted eye-blink code**: Already in use. Yes (1 blink) / No (2 blinks). Functional for basic needs but extremely slow for complex communication.\n- **Eye-gaze letter board**: Communication partner holds a transparent letter board (E-TRAN board) between themselves and the patient. Letters are arranged in groups at the corners and center. Patient looks at the group containing the desired letter, then at the specific letter. Partner reads the selection. Can achieve 2-4 words per minute with practice.\n- **Vertical scanning alphabet board**: Letters presented sequentially on a vertical display. Patient blinks at the desired letter. More independent than E-TRAN but slow.\n\n**Step 3: High-Technology AAC (Goal)**:\n\n1. **Eye-tracking computer systems**: \n- **Tobii Dynavox** (e.g., Tobii I-Series or TD Pilot): Eye-tracking device mounted to a computer screen. Infrared cameras track the patient\'s gaze position. He looks at letters, words, or icons on screen to compose messages. Text-to-speech output.\n- Requires calibration for vertical gaze (most systems optimize for both horizontal and vertical — may need customization since he only has vertical).\n- Communication rates: 8-15 words per minute with practice.\n- Can also control computer, access internet, email, social media — restoring cognitive participation.\n\n2. **Eye-blink switches**:\n- If eye-tracking calibration fails due to absent horizontal gaze, an infrared eye-blink sensor can serve as a binary switch.\n- Combined with scanning software (letters highlighted sequentially, patient blinks to select), this provides independent communication.\n- Slower than full eye-tracking (3-6 words per minute) but reliable.\n\n3. **Brain-computer interface (BCI)** — emerging technology:\n- EEG-based systems detect cortical signals associated with intended movements or attention.\n- P300 speller: Letters flash on screen, patient attends to desired letter, EEG detects the P300 evoked potential.\n- Investigational but increasingly available in specialized centers.\n\n**Step 4: Assessment by SLP and Assistive Technology Specialist**:\n- Formal AAC evaluation by SLP with AT expertise\n- Trial of multiple devices to determine optimal system\n- Training patient AND family/caregivers in the selected system\n- Funding: AAC devices are covered by Medicare as durable medical equipment (DME) with proper documentation of medical necessity. Typical cost \$8,000-\$15,000 for eye-tracking system.',
