    Section(
      title: '+ Outline the rehabilitation management priorities for locked-in syndrome, including ventilator management, contracture prevention, and discharge planning.',
      content:
          '**Inpatient Rehabilitation Priorities**:\n\n**1. Communication (Highest Priority)**:\n- Establish reliable AAC system as detailed in Domain B\n- Every clinical decision must include the patient\'s input via his communication system\n- Document his communication method in the medical record so all staff can interact with him\n- Train all nursing staff, therapists, and family in the eye-blink code\n\n**2. Ventilator Weaning**:\n- Current: tolerating 30-60 minute tracheostomy collar trials\n- **Progressive ventilator free breathing (PVFB)**: Increase collar trial duration by 15-30 minutes per session, 2-3 times daily, monitoring SpO2, HR, respiratory distress\n- Goal: Extend to daytime ventilator independence, then nighttime\n- Complete ventilator liberation is achieved in **some** locked-in patients (approximately 50-60%) as brainstem respiratory centers in the tegmentum may be spared\n- Respiratory therapy: Chest physiotherapy, assisted cough techniques (mechanical insufflation-exsufflation), suctioning protocol\n- Monitor for sleep-disordered breathing — central apnea is common after pontine strokes\n\n**3. Contracture Prevention**:\n- **ROM program**: Passive ROM to all joints 2x daily by PT/OT. Focus on ankles (already -10 degrees dorsiflexion), elbows (already -15 degrees extension), shoulders, and hips.\n- **Positioning**: Scheduled position changes every 2 hours. Ankle dorsiflexion splints worn at night. Resting hand splints in functional position.\n- **Standing program**: Tilt table standing beginning at 30 degrees, progressing to 70-80 degrees. Benefits: contracture prevention, bone density, cardiovascular conditioning, psychological well-being.\n- **Serial casting**: If ankle contractures progress despite splinting, serial casting to gradually restore neutral dorsiflexion.\n\n**4. Skin Protection**:\n- Stage 1 sacral pressure injury — requires aggressive prevention of progression\n- Pressure-relieving mattress (alternating pressure or low-air-loss)\n- Turning schedule every 2 hours documented and enforced\n- Nutrition optimization — ensure adequate protein (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day) and calories\n- Daily skin inspection with documentation\n\n**5. Medical Management**:\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Continue enoxaparin. Bilateral SCDs when in bed.\n- **Bladder**: Transition from indwelling catheter to intermittent catheterization to reduce UTI risk. Schedule every 4-6 hours.\n- **Bowel**: Scheduled bowel program (docusate + senna + glycerin suppository every other day). Prevent impaction.\n- **Pain**: Bilateral shoulder pain — likely from capsular stretch in flaccid shoulders. Shoulder supports (lap tray when seated). Gentle ROM within pain-free range.\n- **Depression screening**: PHQ-2 via eye-blink responses weekly. Extremely high risk for depression (estimates 60-70% in locked-in patients).\n\n**6. Discharge Planning**:\n- **Timeline**: Prolonged inpatient rehabilitation — likely 2-4 months minimum\n- **Home modifications**: Hospital bed, Hoyer lift, ventilator setup, suction equipment, AAC device mounting\n- **Caregiver training**: Wife and potentially hired caregivers trained in tracheostomy care, ventilator management, tube feeding, transfers, skin care, ROM exercises, communication system\n- **Home nursing**: Will require 24-hour care — minimum 16 hours/day skilled nursing plus family\n- **Follow-up**: PM&R, pulmonology, neurology, SLP, ophthalmology (corneal protection given absent corneal reflexes)',
