    Section(
      title: '+ What systems-level failures contribute to misdiagnosis of locked-in syndrome as vegetative state, and how can institutional protocols prevent this?',
      content:
          '**The Diagnostic Error**:\n- Locked-in syndrome is **misdiagnosed as vegetative state or coma** in up to **40-50%** of cases initially (Laureys et al., 2005). Average time to correct diagnosis is **2.5 months** in historical series.\n- This patient was misdiagnosed for 9 days — the wife\'s observations were initially dismissed by staff.\n\n**Systems Failures That Contribute to Misdiagnosis**:\n\n1. **Failure to assess consciousness in patients who cannot move**:\n- Standard neurological assessment relies on motor responses (GCS motor score). A patient with no motor output is scored as GCS 3-4 even if fully conscious.\n- Locked-in patients cannot demonstrate awareness through movement — only through vertical eye movements and blinks, which may be missed if not specifically sought.\n\n2. **Failure to listen to family observations**:\n- This patient\'s wife reported he was responsive. Family observations of consciousness are correct in the majority of cases and should trigger formal assessment.\n- Cultural bias: Families are sometimes dismissed as \'in denial\' rather than taken seriously as diagnosticians.\n\n3. **Lack of standardized consciousness assessment protocols in the ICU**:\n- The GCS was not designed to differentiate locked-in syndrome from vegetative state.\n- The **Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRS-R)** is the gold-standard behavioral assessment for disorders of consciousness and includes specific items that can detect locked-in syndrome.\n\n**Institutional Prevention Protocols**:\n\n1. **Mandatory CRS-R assessment**: All patients with suspected disorder of consciousness should receive serial CRS-R assessments by trained clinicians. This scale specifically evaluates for eye-tracking, command-following via eye movements, and communication through eye blinks.\n\n2. **\"Assume consciousness\" protocol**: Until proven otherwise, treat all patients with acute brainstem lesions as potentially conscious. Speak to the patient, explain procedures, provide orientation information.\n\n3. **Family input documentation**: Create a standardized form for family observations of possible consciousness. Mandatory review by the attending physician.\n\n4. **Neurology consultation trigger**: Automatic neurology consultation for any patient with brainstem stroke who appears unresponsive, specifically to evaluate for locked-in syndrome.\n\n5. **Staff education**: Annual training on differentiating vegetative state, minimally conscious state, and locked-in syndrome. Include nursing staff — they spend the most time at bedside and are most likely to notice subtle signs.\n\n6. **EEG in early evaluation**: Locked-in patients have a **normal or near-normal EEG** (reactive posterior dominant rhythm), unlike vegetative state patients. Early EEG can provide immediate evidence of cortical consciousness.\n\n7. **Incident reporting**: This misdiagnosis should be reported through the hospital\'s patient safety reporting system as a diagnostic error. Root cause analysis should be performed.',
