    Section(
      title: '+ How do you respond to a cognitively intact locked-in patient who communicates a desire to die by withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment, with his distraught family present?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Honor the communication. Do not dismiss or redirect.**\n[Maintain eye contact with the patient. He spent 20 minutes composing this message — it is deliberate and important.]\n"I hear you. You spent a long time telling me this, and I respect that you want me to understand how you feel. I am going to take this seriously, and I am not going to dismiss what you said."\n\n**2. Attend to the wife\'s acute distress without abandoning the patient.**\n[Turn briefly to wife] "Mrs. [Name], I know this is devastating to hear. I want you to know that your husband expressing this does not mean a decision has been made. It means he is suffering and he trusts us enough to be honest about it. I need to talk with him about this, and I would like you to stay if you can, but if you need a moment, our social worker is right outside."\n[If daughter approaches] "I think it might be best if [daughter] stays in the family lounge for now. Can we have someone sit with her?"\n\n**3. Assess the nature of the request — is this depression, suffering, or an autonomous, enduring wish?**\n"[Patient name], I want to understand what you are feeling. I am going to ask you some yes/no questions.\n- Are you in physical pain right now? [Blink response]\n- Are you feeling hopeless about the future? [Blink response]\n- Do you feel like a burden to your family? [Blink response]\n- Have you felt this way since you woke up in the ICU, or is this a recent feeling? [Complex — may need reformulation]\n- If we could find a way to communicate more easily, connect you to the world, and manage your discomfort, would that change how you feel? [Blink response]\n- Have you thought about this request for a long time, or did something happen today that made it worse? [Reformulate as needed]"\n\n**4. Acknowledge the legitimacy of his suffering AND his autonomy.**\n"You are a 48-year-old man who cycled 50 miles for fun, who wrote software, who held his daughter. And now you cannot move, you cannot speak, and you are breathing through a machine. I cannot imagine what that is like, and I am not going to pretend I can. Your feelings make sense. You have every right to feel this way."\n\n**5. Provide honest context about the trajectory — locked-in syndrome has a unique prognosis.**\n"I also want to tell you something that I think matters. You are 6 weeks out from your stroke. Most people with your condition at 6 weeks feel exactly the way you do. But research on locked-in syndrome survivors — people who have lived with this for years — shows something surprising. The majority report meaningful quality of life. Many report life satisfaction similar to healthy individuals. This is not intuitive, and I am not saying it to dismiss your suffering. I am saying that how you feel right now, at 6 weeks, may not be how you feel at 6 months or 6 years. And the technology we are going to give you — the ability to communicate freely, to use a computer, to write to your daughter, to connect with the world — that changes things."\n\n**6. Clarify the ethical and legal framework.**\n"As your physician, I want you to know your rights. You are a competent adult. You have the legal right to refuse any medical treatment, including the ventilator. That is not in question. But a decision like this should not be made during a crisis, and you are in a crisis right now — you are 6 weeks into the most devastating experience of your life, you do not yet have the communication tools you need, and you have not had a chance to see what your life can look like with proper support. I am asking you — not telling you — to give us time. Let us get you the eye-tracking computer. Let us get you talking to a psychologist who specializes in this. Let us get you home. And then, if you still feel this way, we will have that conversation again, and I will respect your decision."\n\n**7. Involve ethics and psychiatry — not as gatekeepers but as support.**\n"I am going to do two things. First, I am going to ask our psychiatrist to meet with you — not to override you, but to make sure depression is not driving this decision. Depression is treatable, even in your situation. Second, I am going to request an ethics consultation, which is standard whenever a patient considers withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment. This is to protect YOUR interests and make sure your voice is heard clearly."\n\n**8. Return to the wife.**\n"Mrs. [Name], your husband is suffering, and he is being honest about it. That honesty is a sign of trust in you and in us. Right now, no decisions are being made. What we are going to do is get him better tools to communicate, address his depression, and give him time to experience life outside this hospital. He deserves to make this decision from the best possible position, not the worst."\n\n**Key Ethical Principles**: Respect for autonomy (he is competent and has the right to refuse treatment). Beneficence (treating depression and providing communication tools may alleviate the suffering driving the request). Non-maleficence (acting on a request driven by treatable depression would cause harm). Temporality (decisions about life-sustaining treatment should not be made in the acute phase when the patient has not yet experienced their fullest potential recovery and adaptation). The goal is not to override his autonomy but to ensure the decision, if ultimately made, is informed, stable, and not driven by a treatable condition.',
